e const _default: any; export default _default` | **3 lines. Types the entire root entry as `any`.** Doesn't declare `split`, `hydrationReady`, `RouterDataProvider`, `Head`, `Body`, `MetaTag`, `useCurrentRouteData`, or `useRouterData`. |\n| `src/web-router/types/components/RouterDataProvider.d.ts` | 95 lines — the only substantive file. Declares `RouterContext`, `serverDataFetcher`, `RouterDataProvider`, `useCurrentRouteData`, `useRouterData` + 5 `type`s. | Good content, but unreachable — nothing points to it. Also types `serverDataFetcher` (`:11`) and `RouterContext` (`:8`) as public, cementing the leaks from §1.1. |\n| `src/web-router/types/hooks.d.ts:1` | `useNavigateWithTransition(): NavigateFunction` | Types the dead stub. |\n| `src/web-router/types/context.d.ts:9` | `OneMgRouterContext` | Declares an **internal** context as public API. |\n| `src/web-router/types/components/MetaTag.d.ts:1` | `MetaTag(): React.JSX.Element` | Fine. |\n| `src/web-router/types/utils/metaDataUtils.d.ts:8,15,24` | `mergeHeadElements`, `deleteHeadTagsByDataAttribute`, `getMetaData` | Declares all three internal helpers as public API. |\n\n**Coverage:** web-router only. **Zero** declarations for `./logger`, `./caching`, `./sentry`, `./otel`, `./WebBridge`, `./hooks` (all 18 native hooks), `./router/ClientRouter`, or `./document`.\n\n**JSDoc typedefs** (`@typedef`/`@type`) exist at `src/web-router/context.jsx:4,10` and `src/web-router/components/RouterDataProvider.jsx:16,20,25,42,51,65,168,198,232` — these are what generated the `.d.ts` above. Elsewhere JSDoc is prose-only `@param`/`@returns` (e.g. `src/otel.js:115-121,144-150,234-243`, `src/logger.js:7-15`, `src/web-router/components/Split.jsx:29-36`, `src/server/renderer/document/Body.jsx:5-12`) — useful docs, not machine-checkable types.\n\n**Note the irony:** three of the six CCA templates are TypeScript (`packages/create-catalyst-app/templates/{none,redux,rtk}-ts/tsconfig.json`), so the framework ships TS starters against a fully untyped framework.\n\n**Recommendation before a freeze:** a freeze without types freezes ambiguity. Either (a) generate real `.d.ts` for all 9 entry points and wire `\"types\"` conditions into the exports map, or (b) delete the 6 stale files and `tsconfig.json` so they stop implying a contract that doesn't exist. Option (a) is the one that makes a freeze meaningful.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Naming inconsistencies worth fixing before a freeze\n\n### 5.1 Critical — a filename-casing bug that breaks case-sensitive filesystems\n\nThe file is `src/native/terminalProgress.js` (lowercase `t`; confirmed by `ls` and `git ls-files`), but **all four importers use `TerminalProgress.js`**:\n\n- `src/native/setupEmulatorIos.js:4` — `import TerminalProgress from \"./TerminalProgress.js\"`\n- `src/native/androidSetup.js:5` — `import TerminalProgress from \"./TerminalProgress.js\"`\n- `src/native/buildIos/index.js:6` — `require(\"../TerminalProgress.js\").default`\n- `src/native/buildAndroid/index.js:6` — `require(\"../TerminalProgress.js\").default`\n\nThis works on macOS/Windows (case-insensitive) and **fails with `ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND` on Linux** — i.e. in Docker builds and Linux CI. It breaks `catalyst setupEmulator:ios`, `catalyst setupEmulator:android`, `catalyst buildApp:ios`, and `catalyst buildApp:android` (all four routed through `bin/catalyst.js:15-18`). Note `tsconfig.json:82` sets `forceConsistentCasingInFileNames: true`, which would catch this — but `tsc` is never run (§4). Rename the file to `TerminalProgress.js` to match the exported class name (`terminalProgress.js:3,223`).\n\n### 5.2 `OneMgRouterContext` — company-branded legacy name\n\n`src/web-router/context.jsx:12` and the `oneMgRouterContext` typedef (`:4`). \"1mg\" is the internal company name, inappropriate in an MIT-licensed OSS framework called Catalyst. Used at `MetaTag.jsx:3,12` and `RouterDataProvider.jsx:11,273,290`. Currently internal — but `src/web-router/types/context.d.ts:9` declares it public. Rename to `CatalystRouterContext` (or `RouterMatchContext`) while it's still internal; after a freeze this gets much harder.\n\n### 5.3 Two contexts, near-identical names, different shapes\n\n`RouterContext` (`RouterDataProvider.jsx:22`, holds route data) vs `OneMgRouterContext` (`context.jsx:12`, holds `matchedRoutes`/`refetchData`/`clear`). Both are provided by the same component (`RouterDataProvider.jsx:273-277`). One is publicly exported, one isn't. Names give no hint of the difference. Rename to something like `RouteDataContext` and `RouteMatchContext`.\n\n### 5.4 `./hooks` exports four non-hooks\n\n`requestCameraPermission`, `requestHapticFeedback`, `requestNotificationPermission`, `sanitizeFilePickerOptions` (`hooks/index.js:3,5,6,10`). React's `use*` convention makes an entry point named `hooks` a strong signal. Move the three `request*` imperatives to a `./native` or `./bridge` entry; drop `sanitizeFilePickerOptions` entirely (internal).\n\n### 5.5 Same name, two entry points, different defaults\n\n`requestHapticFeedback` exists at `hooks/useHapticFeedback.js:8` (default `\"light\"`) and as `WebBridge.requestHapticFeedback` at `WebBridge.js:273` (default `\"VIRTUAL_KEY\"`). Likewise `requestCameraPermission` at `hooks/useCamera.js:191` and `WebBridge.js:316`. Same name, different behavior, both public — pick one.\n\n### 5.6 Inconsistent hook option signatures\n\nEleven hooks take `({webFallback} = {})`. The outliers: `useAI(options = {})` (`useAI.js:46`), `useGoogleSignIn(defaultOptions = {})` (`useGoogleSignIn.js:8`), `useNativeTransition(defaults = {})` (`useNativeTransition.js:29`), `useVideoStream({onQRDetected, webFallback} = {})` (`useVideoStream.js:16`), and `useIntent()` / `useNotification()` / `useNotificationPermission()` which take nothing (`useIntent.js:7`, `useNotification.js:114`, `:69`). Three different names for the same first parameter. Standardize on `options` with `webFallback` as a member.\n\n### 5.7 Default exports that hide the real name\n\n- `src/index.jsx:5` — `export { split as default }`. Anonymous default aliasing a named export; unused by every consumer.\n- `src/router/ClientRouter.js:15` — `export default clientRouter`, no named export. Consumers must invent a name (all happen to pick `clientRouter`, `templates/none-js/client/index.js:5`).\n- `src/native/bridge/WebBridge.js:533` — default-exports the *instance*, not the class; the class name and instance name are identical, which reads as a class import at call sites.\n\n### 5.8 Named vs. default export sets disagree\n\n- `./sentry` — `ErrorBoundary` is named-only, missing from the default object (`sentry.js:120-125`).\n- `./otel` — `init` is default-only, never named; `IS_BOT_KEY` is named-only, missing from the default (`otel.js:401`).\n\nPick one convention per entry.\n\n### 5.9 `hydrationReady`\n\n`src/web-router/components/Split.jsx:27`. The name doesn't convey that it must be **awaited before** `hydrateRoot` — it reads like a boolean predicate. Internal comments still call it `loadableReady` (`Split.jsx:13`), a leftover from `@loadable/component`. Consider `whenHydrationReady()` or `awaitHydration()`.\n\n### 5.10 Redundant single-line indirection modules\n\n`src/native/bridge/hooks.js` (`export * from \"./hooks/index.js\"`), `src/caching.js`, `src/router.js`. Each is a one-line re-export creating a second name for the same thing. Point `exports` directly at the real files.\n\n---\n\n## 6. `module-alias` / `@catalyst/template` — is it part of the public contract?\n\n**Yes, unavoidably — and it is the single most fragile, least documented part of the API surface.**\n\n### 6.1 Declaration\n\n`packages/catalyst-core/package.json:16-18`:\n```json\n\"_moduleAliases\": { \"@catalyst/template\": \".\" }\n```\n\nRead at build time from *catalyst-core's own* `package.json` by three separate resolvers:\n- `src/vite/vite.config.js:120-126,134`\n- `src/vite/node-loader.mjs:38-47`\n- `src/vite/inject-cache-key-plugin.js:32-43`\n\nEach resolves the alias value against **`process.env.src_path`** — the *consuming app's* root, not catalyst-core's (`node-loader.mjs:55-57`: `resolvePath(appRoot, ...aliasPath.split(\"/\"))`; `vite.config.js:192`; `inject-cache-key-plugin.js:49`). So `\".\"` maps `@catalyst/template` → the consumer's project root.\n\n### 6.2 Direction of dependency — this is the crux\n\n`@catalyst/template` is **not** an alias consumers use to reach the framework. It is how **the framework reaches back into the consumer's app** — an inverted, implicit dependency. Every one of the 8 uses is inside catalyst-core:\n\n| Framework file | Line | Imports from the app |\n|---|---|---|\n| `src/server/renderer/handler.jsx` | `:28` | `@catalyst/template/server/document` |\n| `src/server/renderer/handler.jsx` | `:30` | `@catalyst/template/src/js/containers/App/index` |\n| `src/server/renderer/handler.jsx` | `:31` | `@catalyst/template/src/js/routes/utils` → `getRoutes` |\n| `src/server/renderer/handler.jsx` | `:32` | `@catalyst/template/src/js/store/index.js` → `createStore` |\n| `src/server/renderer/handler.jsx` | `:80` | `@catalyst/template/server/index.js` (dynamic) |\n| `src/router/ServerRouter.js` | `:2` | `@catalyst/template/src/js/routes/utils` |\n| **`src/router/ClientRouter.js`** | **`:2`** | **`@catalyst/template/src/js/routes/utils` → `preparedRoutes`** |\n| `src/vite/vite.config.js` | `:67` | `@catalyst/template/src/js/store/index.js` |\n\n**`src/router/ClientRouter.js:2` is the one that leaks into the published API.** `./router/ClientRouter` is entry #4 in the exports map and is imported by all 6 CCA templates (`templates/{none,redux,rtk}-{js,ts}/client/index.js:5-6`), `apps/catalyst-core-test/client/index.js:5`, and both examples. That published module contains a bare `@catalyst/template/...` specifier that **no standard resolver can resolve**. It only works because the app's build runs Catalyst's own Vite config / Node loader.\n\n### 6.3 The implied file-layout contract\n\nBecause the framework imports these paths, every consumer **must** provide, at exact paths:\n\n| Required path | Required export |\n|---|---|\n| `server/document.js` | default component |\n| `server/index.js` | server hooks |\n| `src/js/containers/App/index` | default component |\n| `src/js/routes/utils` | `getRoutes`, `preparedRoutes` |\n| `src/js/store/index.js` | default `createStore` |\n\nConfirmed by every template: `templates/none-js/src/js/routes/utils.js:12` (`preparedRoutes`) and `:40` (`getRoutes`), `templates/none-js/src/js/containers/App/index.js:16`, `templates/common/server/document.js:12`.\n\nThis is a **stricter, harder-to-satisfy public contract than the exports map** — it constrains file paths, filenames, and export names — yet it appears nowhere in the exports map and nowhere in `docs/content/11-API Reference/`. There is no documented list of these five files.\n\n### 6.4 Consumers must also declare six aliases of their own\n\n`src/server/utils/validator.js:80-104` hard-requires six aliases in the consumer's `package.json`:\n\n```js\nconst requiredModuleAliases = {\n    \"@api\": \"api.js\", \"@containers\": \"src/js/containers\", \"@server\": \"server\",\n    \"@config\": \"config\", \"@css\": \"src/static/css\", \"@routes\": \"src/js/routes/\",\n}\n```\n\nMissing any → `\"${key} module alias not defined inside package.json\"` (`:97`). Present in all six templates (`templates/none-js/package.json:16-23`, etc.), `apps/catalyst-core-test/package.json:11`, `examples/useai/package.json:21`, `examples/test-video-hook-poc/package.json:18`.\n\n`validator.js:95-96` additionally forbids any alias key containing `\"catalyst\"` — a namespace reservation, but the check is a substring test on the **key** while the framework's own reserved key is `@catalyst/template`, so a consumer defining `@catalyst/anything` is rejected wholesale. Undocumented.\n\nNote `handleError` (`validator.js:3-5`) only `console.log`s and the function returns `undefined` — validation failures don't throw, they warn and continue.\n\n### 6.5 Precedence bug across the three resolvers\n\nThe same alias merge is written **two different ways**:\n\n| File | Line | Merge order | Winner |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `src/vite/vite.config.js` | `:134` | `{ ..._moduleAliases, ...catalyst_moduleAliases }` | **catalyst-core wins** |\n| `src/vite/node-loader.mjs` | `:47` | `{ ...catalystModuleAliases, ...appModuleAliases }` | **app wins** |\n| `src/vite/inject-cache-key-plugin.js` | `:43` | `{ ...catalystModuleAliases, ...appModuleAliases }` | **app wins** |\n\nBoth `node-loader.mjs:46` and `inject-cache-key-plugin.js:42` carry the comment `// Application aliases take precedence over catalyst-core aliases`. `vite.config.js:134` has no comment and does the opposite. An app that redefines `@catalyst/template` gets different resolution in the Vite client build vs. the Node SSR loader — silent, hard-to-debug divergence. Fix before freezing.\n\n### 6.6 Verdict\n\n`@catalyst/template` **is** part of the public contract, on three counts: it ships inside the published `./router/ClientRouter` entry; it dictates five mandatory app file paths and their export names; and it is coupled to six mandatory consumer-declared aliases enforced at server startup. It is documented in none of these capacities.\n\nBefore a freeze, either (a) document the alias, the five required files, and the six required aliases as a first-class part of the API and fix the §6.5 precedence bug, or (b) invert the dependency — have the app pass `{routes, App, document, createStore, serverHooks}` explicitly into a Catalyst bootstrap function — so the framework stops reaching into consumer source by path. Option (b) also fixes `ClientRouter`'s unresolvable specifier and its module-scope service-worker side effect (§1.4).\n\n---\n\n## Prioritized pre-freeze punch list\n\n| # | Item | Ref |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | `catalyst-core/universal` in the published Quick Start does not exist — broken copy-paste for every new user | §2.2 |\n| 2 | `./caching` r