            "content": "# API freeze at the 1.0 RC cut — decisions, cleanups, and sequencing\n\n> Supporting discussion for Story #341 (Epic #329). Per RFC #177, tagging `1.0.0-rc.1` is the moment we commit to API stability: everything reachable from the exports map becomes semver-protected, and later breaking changes need a major bump plus an RFC. This doc is the result of a full audit of the actual export surface at `0.3.0-beta.4` — what we'd be freezing today, what we should fix first, and the four decisions the team needs to make.\n\n## Context\n\n- The published surface is 9 export entries, but the *real* surface is much larger: the root entry wildcard re-exports all of `react-router-dom` (~76 names). Consumers (docs, templates, examples, apps) use only **13 names from the root** and 6 router names in total.\n- Two exported entry points are broken today: `./caching` re-exports `@tata1mg/api-caching`, which is not a dependency and not installed anywhere (import throws), and the Quick Start docs teach `import { Cache } from 'catalyst-core/universal'`, a specifier that doesn't exist.\n- The package ships **no working TypeScript types** (no `types` field; six stale `.d.ts` files, with the root entry typed as `any`), while three of the six CCA starter templates are TypeScript.\n- Beyond the exports map there is a second, undocumented contract: the `@catalyst/template` alias lets the framework import five files by exact path from the consumer's app, and the server validator requires six aliases in the app's `package.json`. CCA scaffolds all of this, so every real app satisfies it — but it's frozen in practice whether we document it or not.\n\nThe freeze is therefore mostly a *shrink-and-repair* exercise: cut the surface down to what we're willing to support for the life of 1.x, fix what's broken, then lock it with CI.\n\n## Decision 1 — Runtime-adapter seam: in 1.0 or after? (Story #331, gates the RC)\n\n**What this question actually means.** Core today reaches directly for Node/Express primitives: `react-dom/server`'s Node streaming API, a baked-in Express middleware stack, winston file logging via a `global.logger` side effect, `fs` reads in the render path, `process.env` at module init, and a `module-alias` require patch. #315/#316 propose putting a small interface (\"runtime adapter\") between framework logic and those environment specifics, with Node as the default adapter — so Cloudflare Workers/Deno/Bun become adapters instead of forks. Story 1 asks: **is that adapter interface part of the API we freeze at 1.0, or do we ship 1.0 without it?**\n\n- **Option A — Defer; freeze narrow.** Ship 1.0 with today's Node-only architecture, but keep the freeze scoped to the app-facing surface so the seam can arrive in a 1.x minor as an additive API. Fastest path to RC. Risk: if the seam later needs to change an already-frozen entry point, that change waits for 2.0.\n- **Option B — Include the seam in 1.0.** Design and land the adapter interface (8 extension points in #316) before the RC. The 1.0 API is born runtime-agnostic and we never freeze Express-coupled behavior. Cost: this is weeks of design + implementation and pushes the RC out significantly; the interface itself would then be frozen on first release, with little bake time.\n- **Option C — Defer the seam, but don't freeze its blast radius.** Ship 1.0 on Node, and mark the entry points the seam would reshape — `./logger`, `./otel`, and transport-coupled APIs like `responseFlushMiddleware` (which contractually must be mounted immediately before `compression()`) — as **experimental/unstable in 1.0**, excluded from the semver guarantee. The stable core (routing, data fetching, `split`, document, native hooks/WebBridge) freezes now; the seam can land in 1.x without a major.\n\nOption C keeps the RC date close to Option A while removing most of Option A's risk. Whichever way we go, Story 1 must be answered before the exports map is locked, because it determines whether `./logger`/`./otel` are inside or outside the guarantee.\n\n## Decision 2 — What router surface does 1.0 promise?\n\n`src/index.jsx:1` re-exports everything from `react-router-dom@^6`, including 12 `UNSAFE_*`/`unstable_*` names. Freezing that wildcard makes RRD v6's API *our* API — and any RRD major bump a Catalyst breaking change. Note PR #399 (RRD v6 → v7, CVE fixes) is in flight and changes this surface, so this decision also decides #399's fate relative to the RC.\n\n- **Option A — Curated re-export.** Replace the wildcard with an explicit list of vetted names (consumers today use `Link`, `Outlet`, `RouterProvider`, `useParams`, `useNavigate`, plus `createBrowserRouter` internally). Breaking now, while 0.x still allows it; keeps upgrade freedom later (we can hold the curated names stable across an RRD major). Migration cost: near zero for our own templates/docs; unknown external apps importing other RRD names via `catalyst-core` would need a one-line import change.\n- **Option B — Peer dependency, no re-export.** Apps import `react-router-dom` directly; Catalyst declares it a peer dep and only exports its own components/hooks. Cleanest ownership boundary and smallest frozen surface, but the largest migration (every template, doc, and app touches imports), and apps can now drift to RRD versions we haven't validated.\n- **Option C — Keep the wildcard.** Zero migration now; we freeze ~76 names we don't control, including explicitly-unstable ones, and RRD majors become Catalyst majors for the life of 1.x.\n\nEither A or B should land **after** #399 (so we freeze against v7, not v6). C effectively forces #399 to land before RC too — otherwise the v7 bump is a post-freeze breaking change.\n\n## Decision 3 — How do we produce the required TypeScript definitions?\n\nThe freeze requires shipping types; today there are none that work. A freeze without types freezes ambiguity — the `.d.ts` we ship at RC *is* the contract in editors.\n\n- **Option A — JSDoc + `tsc` emit.** Type the public modules via JSDoc (the web-router already has real typedefs), generate declarations with the existing `tsconfig.json` (`emitDeclarationOnly` is already configured, just never run), add `\"types\"` conditions per exports-map entry, and verify in CI. No source rewrite; types can't silently drift from the JS.\n- **Option B — Hand-written `.d.ts` per entry point.** Fastest to ship and gives the cleanest-looking types, but drifts unless CI enforces it (e.g. `tsd` assertion tests + `@arethetypeswrong/cli`). The six existing stale files show exactly how this path decays.\n- **Option C — Migrate public modules to TypeScript.** Strongest guarantee, largest diff; realistically pushes the RC out and churns every file we're trying to stabilize.\n\nWhichever option wins, the exports map gets per-entry `\"types\"` conditions and CI runs `@arethetypeswrong/cli` plus a type-level test so declarations can't rot again. A and B can also be staged: hand-write the small entries now, JSDoc-generate the big ones.\n\n## Decision 4 — The `@catalyst/template` app contract: document and harden it\n\nThe framework imports five files from the consumer's app by exact path (`server/document.js`, `server/index.js`, `src/js/containers/App/index`, `src/js/routes/utils` with `getRoutes`/`preparedRoutes`, `src/js/store/index.js`), and the server validator requires six aliases (`@api`, `@containers`, `@server`, `@config`, `@css`, `@routes`) in the app's `package.json`. CCA scaffolds every one of these, so this **is already our de-facto contract** — the proposal is not to change the design, but to make sure it can't cause issues once frozen:\n\n1. **Fix the resolver precedence bug** before RC: `src/vite/vite.config.js:134` merges aliases with catalyst-core winning, while `src/vite/node-loader.mjs:47` and `src/vite/inject-cache-key-plugin.js:43` merge with the app winning (both carry a comment saying the app should win). An app that redefines an alias silently gets different resolution in the client build vs SSR. Pick app-wins everywhere.\n2. **Document the contract as first-class API**: the five required files + their required export names, and the six required aliases, in the API reference. Frozen at 1.0 like any export.\n3. **Make validation honest**: `src/server/utils/validator.js` currently `console.log`s alias failures and continues; missing-contract errors should fail fast with a clear message (fits Story 6's error-code work, e.g. a `CONFIG-xxx` code).\n4. Inverting the dependency (app passes `{routes, App, store, document}` into a bootstrap function instead of the framework importing by path) is a real improvement but belongs with the runtime-seam work — 1.x/2.0 territory, not a pre-RC change.\n\n## Fix-regardless punch list (no team decision needed; land in 0.4.x while breaking is free)\n\n**Broken today**\n- `./caching` → `@tata1mg/api-caching` is undeclared and uninstalled; the entry throws on import. Remove the entry (or make the package real and declared).\n- Quick Start documents `catalyst-core/universal`, which doesn't exist — every new user's copy-paste fails. Fix the doc (and decide if a `Cache` API actually exists).\n- `otel.init()` never starts: `process.env.OTEL_ENABLE !== true` compares string to boolean, so it always early-returns. The whole `./otel` entry is inert.\n- `src/native/terminalProgress.js` is imported as `TerminalProgress.js` from four files — works on macOS, `ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND` on Linux/Docker, breaking all four native CLI commands.\n\n**Shrink the surface**\n- Remove internal leaks from the root barrel: `serverDataFetcher`, `RouterContext`, `mergeHeadElements`, `deleteHeadTagsByDataAttribute`, `getMetaData`, the dead `useNavigateWithTransition` stub, and the anonymous `export { split as default }`.\n- Drop the `./document` entry (unused by every consumer; duplicated by the root) — or make it canonical and remove the root re-export.\n- `./hooks`: drop `sanitizeFilePickerOptions` (internal helper); decide where the three imperative `request*` functions live (an entry named `hooks` exporting non-hooks is a trap).\n- `WebBridge`: the exported singleton exposes `_logInitialization`, `testCallback`, `debug`, `getHandler`, and both legacy device-info paths as enumerable public members. Make private with `#fields` before they're frozen.\n- Delete dead files (~1,700 LOC, zero importers): `native/bridge/standardizedHooks.js` (duplicate name-colliding hooks), `router.js` (legacy `@tata1mg/router` shim), `native/bridge/utils/NativeBridge.js`, `utils/CameraUtils.js`, `bridge/errors.js`, `bridge/safeArea.js`.\n\n**Finalize names / consistency**\n- Rename `OneMgRouterContext` (company-branded) while it's still internal; disambiguate it from the near-identical `RouterContext`.\n- Standardize hook option signatures (eleven hooks take `{webFallback}`; `useAI`/`useGoogleSignIn`/`useNativeTransition`/`useIntent` diverge).\n- Reconcile duplicate APIs with different defaults: `requestHapticFeedback` defaults to `\"light\"` via `./hooks` but `\"VIRTUAL_KEY\"` via `WebBridge` — same name, different behavior.\n- Align named vs default exports: `./sentry`'s default omits `ErrorBoundary`; `./otel`'s `init` is default-only.\n\n**Declare the undeclared**\n- `@sentry/node`, `@sentry/react`, 11× `@opentelemetry/*`, and `catalyst-ai` are imported but undeclared — declare as `peerDependencies` + `peerDependenciesMeta: { optional: true }` so installs are honest. Add null-guards where absence currently throws (`captureException` et al.).\n- `examples/useai` still imports from `@tata1mg/router`, contradicting our own 0.3.x migration docs — migrate or de-list it as a reference.\n\n## How the freeze is enforced (once decisions land)\n\n- **Exports-map lock**: a unit test that imports every exports-map entry and snapshots `Object.keys()` of each — any accidental addition/removal/rename fails CI (fits Story 3's CI work).\n- **Types lock**: `\"types\"` condition per entry + `@arethetypeswrong/cli` + type assertion tests in CI.\n- **Process lock**: after `1.0.0-rc.1`, a PR that changes the snapshot requires an accepted RFC (documented in CONTRIBUTING, enforced by the failing snapshot).\n- **Sequencing**: Story 1 decision → land punch list + Decisions 2/4 as `0.4.0` → types (Decision 3) → surface-adding in-flight PRs (#328 custom API routes, #254/#249/#239 native hooks) either land before RC or explicitly wait for 1.1 → land #399 → cut `1.0.0-rc.1`.\n\n## Discussion ask\n\n1. **Decision 1** (gates everything): A, B, or C on the runtime seam? If C, confirm `./logger` + `./otel` as the experimental set.\n2. **Decision 2**: curated re-export, peer dep, or keep the wildcard — and therefore does #399 land pre-RC (it should in all cases)?\n3. **Decision 3**: JSDoc-generated, hand-written, or TS migration for the shipped types?\n4. Any objection to the punch list landing as `0.4.0` breaking cleanups? Silence = we proceed.\n",
