            "content": "# Catalyst 1.0 API freeze — discussion\n\nEpic #329 targets a 1.0.0 stable release. Per our release flow (#177), tagging `1.0.0-rc.1` is the point where we commit to API stability: anything public after that only changes with a major bump and an RFC. This doc comes out of a full audit of what `catalyst-core@0.3.0-beta.4` actually exports. Most of what the audit found can be cleaned up without debate; seven things need a team decision first.\n\nWhat we'd be committing to:\n\n- Public API = everything importable from the exports map, plus the project-structure contract that create-catalyst-app scaffolds (decision 4).\n- Before the tag we should decide what's in, fix what's broken, and shrink the surface to what we'll support for all of 1.x.\n\n## Decisions\n\n### 1. Runtime adapter — in 1.0 or after? (story #331, blocks the rest)\n\nCore currently calls Node and Express directly: winston file logging through a `global.logger` side effect, a baked-in Express middleware stack, Node's react-dom streaming API, `fs` reads during render, `process.env` at import time. #315/#316 propose a small adapter interface so other runtimes (Cloudflare Workers, Deno, Bun) plug in instead of forking. The question is only timing.\n\n- a) Ship 1.0 Node-only, add the adapter in a 1.x minor. Fastest. Risk: if the adapter later needs to change an already-frozen entry point, that waits for 2.0.\n- b) Build the adapter first and freeze it as part of 1.0. Runtime-agnostic from day one, but weeks more work, and the interface freezes with no bake time.\n- c) Ship Node-only, and mark `./logger` and `./otel` experimental (outside the semver guarantee) so the adapter can reshape them in 1.x. Nearly as fast as (a) without its main risk.\n\n### 2. react-router-dom surface\n\n`src/index.jsx` re-exports all of react-router-dom: about 76 names, 12 of them `UNSAFE_*`/`unstable_*`. Our own docs, templates and examples use six. Whichever way we go, PR #399 (v6 → v7) should land before the freeze so we don't freeze v6.\n\n- a) Re-export a curated list (`Link`, `Outlet`, `RouterProvider`, `useParams`, `useNavigate`, and a few more). A small break now, while 0.x still allows it; router upgrades stay in our hands.\n- b) Stop re-exporting; apps import react-router-dom directly and we declare it a peer dependency. Cleanest boundary, biggest migration across templates and docs.\n- c) Keep the wildcard. No migration, but every router major becomes a Catalyst major for the life of 1.x.\n\n### 3. TypeScript definitions\n\nThere are no working types today: no `types` field, and the one substantial `.d.ts` types the root entry as `any`. Three of our six starter templates are TypeScript. Whatever we ship at rc.1 is the contract people see in their editors.\n\n- a) Type the public modules with JSDoc and generate `.d.ts` with tsc (the tsconfig for this already exists, it's just never run). No source rewrite, and types can't drift from the code.\n- b) Hand-write one `.d.ts` per entry point. Fastest to ship; needs CI checks to stay honest.\n- c) Convert the public modules to TypeScript. Strongest guarantee, largest diff, likely pushes the RC out.\n\n### 4. The app contract\n\nThe framework imports five files from the consumer's app by exact path (`server/document.js`, `server/index.js`, `src/js/containers/App`, `src/js/routes/utils`, `src/js/store`) and requires six aliases in the app's package.json. CCA scaffolds all of it, so this is already our contract in practice — the proposal is to keep the design and make it safe to freeze:\n\n- Fix the alias-precedence bug: the Vite client build resolves with core's aliases winning, the SSR loader with the app's winning. Pick app-wins everywhere.\n- Document the five files and six aliases in the API reference, as normal public API.\n- Make the startup validator fail with a clear error instead of logging and continuing.\n\nInverting the design (the app hands routes/App/store/document to a bootstrap call instead of the framework importing them) is worth doing, but alongside the adapter work in 1.x — not before the RC.\n\n### 5. Which in-flight PRs make 1.0\n\nEach of these adds public API. Landed before rc.1, it freezes immediately with no bake time; otherwise it ships in a 1.x minor. Needs a call per PR:\n\n- #328 custom API routes\n- #254 web-fallback hooks + `useNativeTransition`\n- #249 biometric login / credential storage\n- #239 `useVideoStream`\n- #261 parallel fetches + router-level caching\n\n### 6. Caching — 1.0 feature or not?\n\n`./caching` re-exports `@tata1mg/api-caching`, which is neither declared nor installed — importing it throws today. The Quick Start also teaches `catalyst-core/universal`, which doesn't exist. Either caching becomes a real, declared, frozen 1.0 feature, or we drop the entry and reintroduce it in 1.x. The docs fix happens either way.\n\n### 7. React version policy\n\n`react` and `react-dom` are exact-pinned regular dependencies (19.0.0). Freezing that means Catalyst owns the app's React version, and the next React major reopens the question mid-1.x. Options: switch to peer dependencies with a supported range, or keep the pin. Related: `useAI` depends on `catalyst-ai`, which is at 0.1.0 — probably belongs in the experimental tier rather than the frozen surface.\n\n## Agreed cleanup (lands in 0.4.x either way)\n\nNo decision needed; listed so nothing lands by surprise.\n\n- Fix `otel.init()` — a string-vs-boolean env check makes the whole `./otel` entry a no-op today.\n- Fix the `terminalProgress.js` import casing that breaks all four native CLI commands on Linux/Docker.\n- Remove internals from the root barrel: `serverDataFetcher`, `RouterContext`, the meta-tag helpers, the dead `useNavigateWithTransition` stub, and the anonymous `split` default export.\n- Drop the `./document` entry (unused; duplicated by the root).\n- Hide WebBridge's internals — `_logInitialization`, `testCallback`, `debug`, `getHandler`, and the legacy device-info paths are all