          "text": "It's about which direction the wiring flows between the framework and the app.\n\n**Today: the framework imports the app.** Core's code literally contains import statements that reach into your app's folder, like this (from `src/server/renderer/handler.jsx`):\n\n```js\nimport App from \"@catalyst/template/src/js/containers/App/index\"\nimport { getRoutes } from \"@catalyst/template/src/js/routes/utils\"\n```\n\n`@catalyst/template` is an alias pointing at the app's root folder. So the framework grabs your App component, routes, store and document by *filesystem path*. That's exactly why every app must have those five files at those exact paths with those exact export names: move or rename one, and the framework's own imports break. It's also why the alias system (and its precedence bug) has to exist at all.\n\n**The redesign: the app hands its pieces to the framework.** The imports flip direction. Your app's entry file would do something like:\n\n```js\nimport { createCatalystApp } from \"catalyst-core\"\nimport App from \"./src/js/containers/App\"\nimport { getRoutes } from \"./src/js/routes/utils\"\nimport createStore from \"./src/js/store\"\nimport Document from \"./server/document\"\n\ncreateCatalystApp({ App, routes: getRoutes(), createStore, document: Document })\n```\n\nThe framework never imports from the app anymore; it just receives everything as arguments to one bootstrap call. Then the fixed file layout stops mattering (put your files wherever you want), the `@catalyst/template` alias and its bug disappear, and standard bundlers can resolve everything without Catalyst's custom loader. This is how most frameworks do it.\n\n**Why it's option (b) and not the recommendation:** getting there means rewriting core's server and renderer entry points, updating all six create-catalyst-app templates, rewriting the docs, and breaking every existing app's entry files, all right before the RC whose purpose is stability. So the doc recommends keeping